### 2.16 `renderFinderUser` contract

```ts
type FinderUserVars = {
    task:string          // --task, default "(not provided)"
    survey:boolean
    diff:string          // diff mode only; "" in survey
    fileContext:string   // diff mode only; "" in survey
    manifest:string      // survey only; "" in diff mode
}
```

No value is defanged or escaped; all four strings are inserted raw. The
output, with `T[n]` meaning line `n` of the baseline `finder_user.tmpl`
(1-based, without its newline), is exactly:

```
T[1] + "\n\n" + task + "\n"
+ (survey
    ? "\n" + T[5] + "\n\n" + T[7] + "\n\n<manifest>\n" + manifest
      + "\n</manifest>\n\n" + T[13] + "\n"
    : "\n" + T[15] + "\n\n<diff>\n" + diff + "\n</diff>\n\n" + T[21]
      + "\n\n<files>\n" + fileContext + "\n</files>\n\n" + T[27] + "\n")
+ "\n"
```

`T[1]` is the task heading, `T[5]` the manifest heading, `T[7]` the
"contents are not inline" sentence, `T[13]` the survey grounding sentence,
`T[15]` the changes heading, `T[21]` the files-touched heading, `T[27]` the
diff-mode grounding sentence. Copy those lines byte for byte (they contain
U+2014 characters; keep them). Survey selection: `survey` is true exactly
when `--scope repo`.

Finder inputs from the CLI: in survey, `{task, survey:true, manifest}`; in
diff mode, `{task, diff, fileContext}` where `diff` is the fitted
(possibly truncated) scope diff and `fileContext` is the context area's
numbered file context bounded by `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes`. Before any
model call the CLI rejects `byteLength(system) + byteLength(user)` (UTF-8
bytes) above `limits.max_prompt_bytes` as a usage error (CLI area owns the
message).

### 2.17 `renderVerifierUser` contract and `data` defanging

```ts
type VerifierVars = {
    severity:string      // finder's severity (canonical after validate)
    confidence:number
    file:string
    lines:string         // formatted by the verifier area (lineRange)
    description:string
    codeQuote:string
    fixedCode:string
    fileSnippet:string   // numbered snippet from the context area
}
```

The finder's `reasoning` is deliberately absent (anti-anchoring). The
output is the baseline `verifier_user.tmpl` text (25 lines, trailing
newline kept) with each action replaced in place, no other whitespace
changes:

| Template action                  | Replacement                          |
|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `{{data .Severity}}`             | `dataText(severity)`                 |
| `{{printf "%.2f" .Confidence}}`  | `formatFixed2(confidence)`           |
| `{{data .File}}`                 | `dataText(file)`                     |
| `{{data .Lines}}`                | `dataText(lines)`                    |
| `{{data .Description}}`          | `dataText(description)`              |
| `{{data .CodeQuote}}`            | `dataText(codeQuote)`                |
| `{{data .FixedCode}}`            | `dataText(fixedCode)`                |
| `{{data .FileSnippet}}`          | `dataText(fileSnippet)`              |

The final line (the adjudication instruction) must follow every untrusted
block.

`dataText(s)` defangs closing wrapper tags so untrusted text cannot end its
own tag early. Go pattern:
`(?i)</\s*(claim|code_quote|fixed_code|file_contents)\b`, each match
replaced by `<\/` + the captured name (original case kept; the whitespace
between `</` and the name is removed). Example: `</CODE_QUOTE >` becomes
`<\/CODE_QUOTE >`. Only closing tags are touched; opening tags are not.

Exact semantics to reproduce (Go RE2 differs from JS defaults):

- `\s` is ASCII only: `[\t\n\f\r ]` (no `\v`, no Unicode spaces).
- Case-insensitive matching uses Unicode simple case folding: `ſ`
  (U+017F) matches `s`, `K` (U+212A) matches `k`. In JS this needs the
  `u` and `i` flags.
- `\b` is an ASCII word boundary: the character after the name must be
  absent or not in `[0-9A-Za-z_]`. With `iu` flags JS treats `ſ` and `K`
  as word characters, so do not use JS `\b`; check the following code
  unit explicitly.

A conforming implementation:

```ts
const CLOSE = /<\/[\t\n\f\r ]*(claim|code_quote|fixed_code|file_contents)/giu
function dataText(s:string):string {
    return s.replace(CLOSE, (m, name:string, at:number, all:string) => {
        const next = all.charCodeAt(at + m.length)
        return isAsciiWord(next) ? m : '<\\/' + name
    })
}
```

(No valid match can start inside a rejected candidate, so continuing after
it is equivalent to Go's scan.)

`formatFixed2` (shared `gocompat` module) must equal Go
`fmt.Sprintf("%.2f", x)`: correctly rounded
from the exact binary value with ties to even. `Number.prototype.toFixed`
rounds exact ties up, so it differs on exactly representable ties: the
odd multiples of 0.125 (`0.125 -> "0.12"`, `0.375 -> "0.38"`,
`0.625 -> "0.62"`, `0.875 -> "0.88"`, `1.125 -> "1.12"`). Also Go prints
### 2.13 Go-vs-TS differential harness (temporary)

Purpose: prove parity on observable output by running the baseline Go CLI
and the TS CLI on identical inputs against the same fake inference server.
It lives in `test/diff/` with entry `test/diff/index.ts`, bundled to
`test/diff-bundle.js` by `scripts/bundle.ts --diff`, run by
`npm run test:diff`. It is not imported by `test/index.ts` and is deleted
at M6.

CI runs it from M1 to M5 on Ubuntu only (a job in `ci.yml` with Go set
up, then `npm run build` and `npm run test:diff`), with an allowlist of
the deliberate changes listed in spec.md, "Deliberate changes from the Go
baseline". Differences outside the allowlist fail the job.

Building the Go baseline:

1. Binary cache path: `node_modules/.cache/roast-diff/roast-go-cd08f10`.
   Reuse it if present.
2. Otherwise: `git worktree add --detach <tmp>/roast-go cd08f10`, then in
   it `go build -trimpath -ldflags "-X
   github.com/bluesky-social/roast/internal/buildinfo.Version=baseline" -o
   <cache path> ./cmd/roast` with `CGO_ENABLED=0` and `GOFLAGS` removed
   from the environment, then `git worktree remove --force <tmp>/roast-go`
   (also on failure).
3. If `go` is not on `PATH`, the harness prints a TAP skip for every
   scenario and exits 0 (CI installs Go, so it never skips there).

The TS side is `dist/cli.js` (the harness requires `npm run build` first),
spawned as `node dist/cli.js`, not the in-process `run`, so both CLIs are
exercised as processes.

Per scenario:

1. Start one `node:http` fake server (the same fake the CLI tests use,
   speaking the `chat`, `responses`, and `anthropic` protocols). Responses
   are scripted per scenario and selected by request content (model name
   plus number of conversation turns), never by arrival order, because
   finder and verifier calls may interleave differently.
2. Build two identical fixture repositories, one per CLI, from the same
   function: fixed file contents, fixed `GIT_AUTHOR_DATE` /
   `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE` and identity, so commit IDs match.
3. Write the same `.roast.json` (profiles on the fake's URL, credentials
   via `api_key_env`) into both. Each CLI approves its own copy with
   `trust --yes` first: trust hashes differ by design (v1 vs v2).
4. Run each CLI with a scrubbed environment: no `ROAST_*`, `OPENAI_*`,
   `OPENROUTER_*`, `ANTHROPIC_*`, `DEBUG`; private `HOME` and
   `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`; the profile's key variable set.
5. Compare, after normalization:
   - exit code (exact);
   - stdout when the scenario requests JSON output (parsed, deep equal);
   - requests the fake received, grouped by (model, turn count): method,
# Go notes

Applies to `cmd/roast`, `internal/`, and Go tests. Notes for one area
are in that area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).

## CI checks

- CI runs `go mod tidy` and fails on a diff: run it after removing code
  that was the last user of a dependency.
- CI runs staticcheck (`go tool -modfile=.github/tools/staticcheck/go.mod
  staticcheck ./...`); deleting a caller can leave an unused helper that
  fails U1000.
- `scripts/licenses.sh` special-cases notices for specific linked
  packages. When a dependency drops out of the `./cmd/roast` graph,
  check `go list -deps ./cmd/roast` for each target and update the
  script.
- CI runs `npm run test:go` with `GOFLAGS=-race`, so Go tests that spawn
  `go` subprocesses inherit `-race`; such a subprocess must not also set
  `CGO_ENABLED=0` for a build.

## CLI tests (`internal/cli`)

- Tests drive `ExecuteContext` against an `httptest` fake inference
  server; `executeContext(ctx, args, stdin, stdout, stderr)` lets a test
  answer prompts (`ExecuteContext` passes `os.Stdin`).
- `internal/testutil.ClearAmbientEnvironment` strips `ROAST_*`,
  `OPENAI_*`, `OPENROUTER_*` and `ANTHROPIC_*` in `TestMain`.
- A test that runs a review needs a `.roast.json`
  (`project_config_test.go`: `projectConfig`, `writeFile`, `chdir`,
  `newModelRecorder`). Profiles name a credential with `api_key_env` and
  set it with `t.Setenv`.
- The `httptest` server is a custom endpoint, so a review test approves
  first: `approveProjectConfig(t, ...)` (`trust_test.go`, runs
  `trust --yes`), or `settingsRepo`, which approves. A test asserting a
  config error approves a valid config and then edits it
  (`approvedThenEdited`) so a trust failure cannot mask the error.
- `roast doctor` skips endpoint probes for an unapproved config. A
  doctor test that probes a custom endpoint chdirs into a temp repo and
  approves there (`approveDoctorConfig`, `doctor_test.go`), never in
  this checkout.
- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks
  which file's text reached the fake server keeps decoys out of scope
  (for example via `.git/info/exclude`).
- `settings_test.go` has `requestRecorder` (records model,
  `Authorization`, body), `settingsRepo`, `reviewMeta` (reads the run's
  `meta.json`), and `redirectDefaultTransport`, which swaps
  `http.DefaultTransport` so a profile on a provider's default endpoint
  reaches the fake server.
- `init_test.go` has `providerServer`, a fake speaking the Responses
  (`/v1/responses`), Anthropic (`/v1/messages`), and OpenRouter chat
  (`/api/v1/chat/completions`) protocols; pair it with
  `redirectDefaultTransport(t, server.URL)`.
- Terminal detection goes through the package var
  `interactiveTerminals` (`prompt.go`); tests swap it with
  `simulateTerminals(t, ...)` (`trust_prompt_test.go`).
